# Cover Letter Standards

Canonical template: `1 - Design/cover-letter.html`

## Non-negotiable design

- Gold theme only — `#c8a96e`; no company accent colors or logos
- Fonts — Syne (display) + DM Mono (mono)
- Dark header, light body
- No images — logo slot removed via JS when `--company-logo-url` is empty

## CSS branding block

```css
:root {
  --company-name:        "Company";
  --company-team:        "Team Name";
  --hiring-manager:      "Hiring Manager";
  --role-title:          "Role Title";
  --company-logo-url:    "";
}
```

## Structure

Header → addressee block → role chip → salutation → body (3–5 paragraphs, one pull quote) → signature. The `?continuous` script must be present for single-page PDF export.

## Tone

- Open with a specific story or insight from `candidate-profile.md`, not a generic introduction
- Acknowledge gaps briefly; do not minimize or dwell
- Close with the canonical invitation: “I'd welcome the opportunity to further illustrate how I can add value to the team at &lt;Company&gt;.” (confidence + offer to prove it). Website link is optional; omit when the user asks.
- Must read as a human wrote it:
  - **No em dashes** (`—`). Use a period, comma, colon, or rewrite the sentence.
  - Favor plain, spoken rhythm over polished AI cadence
  - Avoid stock transitions and filler (“What draws me…”, empty “welcome the opportunity to discuss…”, “through-line”, “leverage”, mirrored three-beat lists). The approved closing above is the exception.
  - Do not rewrite the job description back at them; show how *you* have done the work
  - Vary sentence length; avoid symmetric paragraph patterns
